==Old Norse==
{{was fwotd|non|2013|February|5}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|non|gem-pro|*hundaradą}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*hundą}} (< {{der|non|ine-pro|*ḱm̥tóm}}) + {{m|gem-pro|*radą||count}}. Cognate to {{cog|en|hundred||[[short hundred]], 100}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|non|/ˈhũndrɑð/|a=12th Century Icelandic}}

===Number===
{{non-noun|n}}

# a [[long hundred]] (120)

====Usage notes====
Christianity introduced the short hundred (100), but the long hundred remained in use for a long time even after that, during which time hundreds were sometimes distinguished as {{m|non|heil||whole}} or {{m|non|tólfræð||twelve-tenned, duodecimal}} (for 120) or {{m|non|tíræð||ten-tenned, decimal}} (for 100).

